BinSafe: store oCMOB's signed one-bit flags as 0xFFFFFFFF like ZenGin.

WriteArchiveBinsafe::write_bool always wrote 1 for true. ZenGin declares some of
oCMOB's flags as *signed* one-bit bit-fields, so reading a set flag yields -1 and its
archiver stores 0xFFFFFFFF. A census of all 10,279 distinct (class, entry) BOOL keys
across every retail Gothic II BinSafe world (NewWorld, OldWorld, AddonWorld and
DragonIsland) finds exactly three entry names that ever hold a non-0/1 value, and none
of them ever uses 1 for true: `locked` (oCMobContainer 211, oCMobDoor 38), `moveable`
(oCMobSwitch 5) and `focusOverride` (oCMOB 2, oCMobSwitch 2). Every one of the other
10,274 keys is 0 or 1, so the quirk is keyed on the entry name.

`takeable` sits in the same bit-field group but is false in every retail world, so no
evidence exists either way and it is left writing 1 rather than guessed. The ASCII
writer already emits "1" and is left alone.

diff --git a/src/archive/ArchiveBinsafe.cc b/src/archive/ArchiveBinsafe.cc
index f3aa39d..d81511f 100644
--- a/src/archive/ArchiveBinsafe.cc
+++ b/src/archive/ArchiveBinsafe.cc
@@ -351,7 +351,15 @@ namespace zenkit {
 
 	void WriteArchiveBinsafe::write_bool(std::string_view name, bool v) {
 		this->write_entry(name, ArchiveEntryType::BOOL);
-		this->_m_write->write_uint(v ? 1 : 0);
+
+		// Quirk: ZenGin declares some of oCMOB's flags as *signed* one-bit bit-fields, so
+		// reading a set flag yields -1 and its archiver stores 0xFFFFFFFF rather than 1.
+		// Every one of the other 10,274 BOOL keys in the retail worlds is 0 or 1.
+		if (v && (name == "locked" || name == "moveable" || name == "focusOverride")) {
+			this->_m_write->write_uint(0xFFFFFFFFu);
+		} else {
+			this->_m_write->write_uint(v ? 1 : 0);
+		}
 	}
 
 	void WriteArchiveBinsafe::write_color(std::string_view name, Color v) {
